diagnosis.summary:
{
  "observations": [
    {
      "type": "observed_problem",
      "finding": "Several VA-16 replies turn a source feature into an unsupported, decisive judgment. They read like product grading, which conflicts with the stated goal.",
      "witnesses": [
        "The baseline Writer says, “Coding from a phone breaks on interruption, not on editor quality” and calls a service and queue “the line between a usable agent and a demo.”",
        "The source says those recovery features “help work continue through interruptions.” It does not establish either comparison.",
        "The conversational-fragment Writer says “the recovery part is the real product” and presents “android killing the foreground service mid-edit” as the hard problem. The source does not describe that failure."
      ],
      "qualification": "These are factual and social problems in these candidates. A specific technical judgment is not inherently condescending, and the packet does not show that every reader would experience these replies that way."
    },
    {
      "type": "observed_problem",
      "finding": "The added instructions did not resolve the demonstrated VA-16 failures.",
      "witnesses": [
        "The evidence-matching fragment produced “The recovery layer decides whether this is real work or a demo,” which exceeds the source.",
        "The inspection-and-repair fragment produced “running commands from a phone is the easy part” and “the review loop decides this one.” The review found neither comparison established.",
        "The native cycle reports that both candidates were blocked and that the inspection-and-repair candidate did not revise the draft."
      ],
      "qualification": "One rollout per treatment does not establish a failure rate or show why the instructions failed."
    },
    {
      "type": "observed_problem",
      "finding": "The direct conversational fragment produced a more conversational VA-16 reply, but it still introduced a factual mismatch and a possible unsupported history.",
      "witnesses": [
        "The reply says “per-call diff cards.” The source says every tool call has a card, with diff sheets available in chat. Those statements are not equivalent.",
        "“Still wild to me” can imply a continuing reaction. The review flags that history, while the contract permits immediate performed affect."
      ],
      "qualification": "The phrase may be read as casual present affect or as duration. The packet does not establish the intended reading. The separate chat-log judgment does not itself establish prior use."
    },
    {
      "type": "intentional_contract_requirement",
      "finding": "The system requires grounded facts, exact-text review, and publication authority. Those are not defects merely because they block a draft.",
      "witnesses": [
        "The content standard requires external facts to remain “sourced or clearly framed as analysis,” and requires a passing contentReview bound to the exact text, source/evidence snapshot, behavior and persona version.",
        "The review instruction says a source_claim must be “visibly attributed in the actual wording.” The binding code also requires attributed=true and an attribution word near the claim.",
        "The Writer contract says a candidate is not publication authorization. It also permits ordinary source-attributed commentary without independent verification solely because an X post is short."
      ],
      "qualification": "The content standard also says a Reply may rely on visible source context. That does not explicitly exempt a factual source report from the separate visible-attribution requirement. The packet does not settle how those rules should interact for a reply whose source is visibly attached."
    },
    {
      "type": "review_claim_to_check",
      "finding": "Some review entries may overclassify conversational wording as unsupported factual assertion, even though other factual objections are well founded.",
      "witnesses": [
        "For VA-14, the review lists “congrats on 1.6” as an unsupported factual claim. The source itself announces CodeAlta 1.6.0, and the phrase also performs a congratulation.",
        "The same review challenges “an agent-built canvas.” The source says a canvas “can be created with an agent via a plugin.” The draft can imply that this canvas was already built by an agent, so that objection deserves separate consideration.",
        "For the direct VA-16 reply, the review requires attribution for “Fixing a bug from a phone,” although the wording may describe the possibility introduced by the visible post. The separate “per-call diff cards” objection remains substantive."
      ],
      "qualification": "The supplied review instruction requires explicit attribution for source_claim assertions. Disagreeing with a claim classification does not establish that the binding gate would pass a different review, or that publication is authorized."
    },
    {
      "type": "intentional_contract_requirement",
      "finding": "The selected behavior influences what the Writer is asked to do. VA-16 explicitly asks for a workflow judgment, while VA-14 asks for celebration.",
      "witnesses": [
        "VA-16 selects judgment, opinionated_peer and judgment depth, with the reason “State the clearest workflow judgment, preference, or consequence the source actually supports.”",
        "VA-14 selects celebration, enthusiast and social_only, and says “do not force a technical caveat afterward.” Its reply starts “congrats on 1.6.”",
        "The persona permits a systems lens when earned, but warns against “automatic hidden-metric, hidden-boundary, or benchmark-reviewer framing.”"
      ],
      "qualification": "The VA-16 behavior does not authorize unsupported comparisons or require grading. The two cases also have different sources, so they do not isolate behavior selection."
    },
    {
      "type": "observed_mechanism",
      "finding": "The binding code checks review shape and some exact-text constraints, but it does not itself prove that a claim is supported or that all nonnumeric claims were extracted.",
      "witnesses": [
        "checkReviewClaims checks that a reviewed assertion occurs in the draft, the quoted excerpt occurs in a supplied source, quantities match, and source claims contain an attribution cue.",
        "The code checks coverage for factual quantities, but the supplied function does not require coverage of every nonnumeric assertion.",
        "The review request asks the reviewer to extract every externally checkable assertion. Semantic assessment therefore depends on that review, plus any other gates not shown."
      ],
      "qualification": "The attribution regex checks for a nearby word, not whether the attribution is semantically correct. The packet does not show all downstream gates."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "rank": 1,
      "cause": "The VA-16 behavior and repeated systems-judgment context may steer generation toward finding a decisive workflow boundary, even when the source supports only a narrower observation.",
      "evidence": [
        "The baseline thesis says interruption recovery determines success rather than editor quality.",
        "The conversational-fragment Writer still selects recovery as “the real product,” despite instructions to join the author and avoid grading.",
        "The persona contains both a recurring recovery/failure lens and explicit warnings not to force it."
      ],
      "uncertainty": "The packet does not isolate the behavior, persona, Writer contract, model or source. The source itself prominently lists recovery."
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "cause": "The review and binding path may make source-linked social replies harder to pass when reviewers extract contextual wording as independent factual claims.",
      "evidence": [
        "The VA-14 review treats “congrats on 1.6” as unsupported.",
        "The review instruction and binding code require explicit wording for claims classified as source_claim.",
        "The content standard also permits replies to rely on visible source context."
      ],
      "uncertainty": "A strict attribution requirement is intentional for claims actually asserted as unverified facts. The packet does not show whether a correctly scoped review of a source-linked reaction would pass all gates."
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "cause": "The Writer may convert requests for strong judgment into unsupported causal certainty, and the added evidence prompts may be insufficient to prevent that conversion.",
      "evidence": [
        "The evidence-matching treatment still says recovery “decides” whether the product is real work or a demo.",
        "The inspection treatment still says the review loop “decides” the outcome.",
        "The source only claims recovery features help continuation through interruptions."
      ],
      "uncertainty": "The contract already requires supported claims. These samples do not show whether the issue comes from instruction salience, interpretation, generation variability, or another factor."
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "cause": "Review classification and the mechanical interpretation rules may constrain legitimate subjective reactions or flag ambiguity too aggressively.",
      "evidence": [
        "The review flags “still wild to me” as continuing emotional history, while immediate performed affect is allowed.",
        "The binding code accepts interpretation only for certain opening patterns and rejects some product or quantity terms."
      ],
      "uncertainty": "A duration claim can require grounding, and the code's restrictions protect factual claims. The packet does not show a legitimate reaction failing solely because of the interpretation regex."
    }
  ],
  "nextCheck": {
    "action": "Manually audit the existing VA-14 and direct VA-16 reviews against their exact replies, source excerpts, review instructions, and binding code. Do not generate or publish anything for this check.",
    "steps": [
      "Separate social acts, immediate affect, product assertions, and implied history. For each disputed span, record the strongest reasonable reading and the exact source support.",
      "For each genuine source assertion, determine whether the stated visible-attribution rule applies and whether the actual text satisfies the binding code. Separately assess the canvas and per-call-diff wording.",
      "Record which blocks follow from the declared contract, which depend on reviewer classification, and which follow from an unsupported claim. Then compare those results with the VA-16 judgment failures."
    ],
    "discrimination": "If the social spans do not assert independent facts, reviewer extraction is a candidate cause. If genuine assertions fail only for absent attribution, the contract interaction is the immediate cause. If correctly classified claims still exceed the source, generation remains a separate cause.",
    "safeguards": "Keep factual provenance, exact-text independent review, owner-history restrictions, and publication authority intact. The check does not assume a phrase ban or model change."
  },
  "limits": [
    "The packet contains eight outputs across two sources. It has no repeated matched trials, room context, live outcomes, or evidence of follower impact.",
    "The direct and Writer lanes differ in contract, schema and prompt context. Their outputs do not isolate the effect of the conversational fragment.",
    "The one Codex Writer sample differs from the baseline output, but one sample per model cannot establish that a model switch repairs the problem.",
    "The native cycle is an offline report with cached stages and k=1. Its KEEP_BASE result is not proof that the base prompt is generally best.",
    "Only selected authority excerpts and binding code are supplied. Other gates, runtime behavior and actual publication outcomes are unknown."
  ]
}
